> What does the encodeURIComponent do? Does this just create the url `https://graph.mapillary.com/${imageId}?fields=thumb_1024_url`

Yes. It is a template literal, so `${imageId}` is replaced with the actual value.

For example:

```js
const imageId = "339131707727137";

const url = `https://graph.mapillary.com/${encodeURIComponent(
  imageId,
)}?fields=thumb_1024_url`;
```

produces:

```text
https://graph.mapillary.com/339131707727137?fields=thumb_1024_url
```

`encodeURIComponent()` safely encodes special characters that could interfere with the URL.

For example:

```js
encodeURIComponent("abc/123");
```

returns:

```text
"abc%2F123"
```

Since your validation only allows digits, `encodeURIComponent(imageId)` will normally leave the value unchanged. It is still good practice when inserting user-provided values into a URL path.

